Clear the unwritten parts of the signal frame before a suspended thread's stack is scanned.

Symptom. A program evaluates a large Nix expression (about 1.25 GiB of
collector memory), drops every reference to it, and runs GC_gcollect.
With boehmgc-clear-collection-residue.patch the evaluation is freed on
bdwgc 8.2.12, but on Determinate Systems' bdwgc fork it still stayed
alive in most runs. huggorm (Python bindings for Nix) runs that fork in
an SBOM worker on sg1, which grew run after run until the kernel killed
it at its 24 GiB limit (nixidae/nixidae#1624, 2026-11-26).

Cause. GC_suspend_handler records the thread's stack pointer inside the
handler, and GC_push_all_stacks scans from there up. The range includes
the frame the kernel built for the signal. The kernel does not write all
of that frame:

- the gap between the end of the saved FPU state, which ends with the
  4 bytes of FP_XSTATE_MAGIC2, and the red zone below the interrupted
  stack pointer: the state is aligned to 64 bytes;
- the slot of each XSAVE state component in its initial configuration
  (XSTATE_BV bit clear). XSAVE skips it, and on a CPU with AVX-512 the
  ZMM_Hi256 and Hi16_ZMM slots alone are 1.5 KiB.

Both keep whatever the stack held before, often the frames of an
earlier, deeper call on that thread. An old pointer there is read as a
root.

Evidence. A bdwgc build with KEEP_BACK_PTRS and GC_DEBUG clients gave,
from a kept attribute set, a chain of 271 heap objects to
GC_REFD_FROM_ROOT at a stack address. gdb put it on a worker thread
parked in a futex, 0xc0 below its stack pointer: below the 128-byte red
zone, in the signal frame. The root word was 0x00007b86_46505845. Its
low half is FP_XSTATE_MAGIC2 ("EXPF"); its high half is 4 stale bytes.
Together they named an address inside the 4 GiB heap. The word below it
held 0x55555554, the default PKRU, the last component of the XSAVE
area.

Fix. GC_clear_signal_frame_slack runs in the suspend handler, before the
stack pointer is stored. From the ucontext it zeroes the gap above the
saved state up to the red zone, and the slot of each component the
header marks unused (x87 and XMM in the legacy area; the others at the
offsets CPUID leaf 0xD gives). sigreturn does not read either part: it
restores a skipped component to its initial configuration. The code is
for x86-64 Linux with GCC or Clang, where the handler receives the
context; elsewhere it does nothing.

Measured on an AMD EPYC (AVX-512), Determinate's bdwgc, both patches,
an evaluation of every top-level nixpkgs attribute, 4 rounds of
evaluate-close-collect per run, MiB in use after each round:

- clear-collection-residue only: kept in 5 of 5 runs (1240 to 3750)
- with this patch: 4 of 5 runs at 0 to 3 MiB every round; 1 run kept
  one evaluation (1240), flat over its rounds
- the small test (60 000 derivation-shaped sets, 10 rounds): 4 MiB in
  all 30 rounds

The remaining one-in-five root is not found yet; see #1624.

Cost: one memset of at most a few KiB, and a CPUID per unused
component, per thread per collection.

Upstream. bdwgc master at ef728c09 (2026-11-24) has the same handler:
it ignores the context ("We believe that in this case the full context
is already in the signal handler frame") and scans from the handler's
stack pointer. No bdwgc issue covers this (searched 2026-11-26). A C
reproduction: a thread that fills its stack with pointers to a large
object, returns, drops the object, and parks; then GC_gcollect from a
second thread, and GC_is_marked on the object.

Base: bdwgc 8.2.12. The measurements above are from the Determinate copy
of this change; this one is built and run through huggorm's suite.

--- a/pthread_stop_world.c
+++ b/pthread_stop_world.c
@@ -302,6 +302,76 @@
 # define GC_lookup_thread_async GC_lookup_thread
 #endif
 
+#if defined(LINUX) && defined(X86_64) && defined(__GNUC__) \
+    && !defined(NO_SA_SIGACTION)
+# include <cpuid.h>
+# include <string.h>
+# include <ucontext.h>
+  /* The suspended thread's stack is scanned from this handler's frame  */
+  /* up, so the kernel's signal frame is scanned too.  The kernel leaves */
+  /* parts of it unwritten, and they hold whatever the stack held       */
+  /* before: the alignment gap between the end of the saved FPU state   */
+  /* (after FP_XSTATE_MAGIC2, 4 bytes) and the red zone, and the slot   */
+  /* of each state component that XSAVE skipped because it was in its   */
+  /* initial configuration (XSTATE_BV bit clear).  An old pointer there */
+  /* keeps its object, and all that it reaches, alive.  Measured: the 4 */
+  /* stale bytes above FP_XSTATE_MAGIC2 made 0x00007b86_46505845, a     */
+  /* pointer into a 4 GiB heap, that kept a 1.2 GiB Nix evaluation      */
+  /* (huggorm, nixidae/nixidae#1624).                                   */
+  /* Neither part is read back: sigreturn restores a skipped component  */
+  /* to its initial configuration, and the gap is no part of the frame.  */
+  /* Zeroing them changes nothing but what the collector sees.          */
+# define FPSTATE_SW_RESERVED 464 /* struct _fpx_sw_bytes */
+# define FPSTATE_XSAVE_HEADER 512
+# define FP_XSTATE_MAGIC1_VALUE 0x46505853U
+# define SIGNAL_RED_ZONE 128
+
+  STATIC void GC_clear_signal_frame_slack(void *context)
+  {
+    const ucontext_t *uc = (const ucontext_t *)context;
+    unsigned char *fp;
+    unsigned char *above;
+    unsigned char *red_zone;
+    unsigned magic1, extended_size, xstate_size;
+    unsigned long long xfeatures, xstate_bv;
+    unsigned i;
+
+    if (NULL == uc || NULL == uc->uc_mcontext.fpregs) return;
+    fp = (unsigned char *)uc->uc_mcontext.fpregs;
+    memcpy(&magic1, fp + FPSTATE_SW_RESERVED, sizeof(magic1));
+    if (magic1 != FP_XSTATE_MAGIC1_VALUE)
+      return; /* no XSAVE frame: legacy FXSAVE layout */
+    memcpy(&extended_size, fp + FPSTATE_SW_RESERVED + 4,
+           sizeof(extended_size));
+    memcpy(&xfeatures, fp + FPSTATE_SW_RESERVED + 8, sizeof(xfeatures));
+    memcpy(&xstate_size, fp + FPSTATE_SW_RESERVED + 16, sizeof(xstate_size));
+    memcpy(&xstate_bv, fp + FPSTATE_XSAVE_HEADER, sizeof(xstate_bv));
+
+    /* The gap, only when the frame sits just below the red zone.       */
+    above = fp + extended_size;
+    red_zone = (unsigned char *)uc->uc_mcontext.gregs[REG_RSP]
+                - SIGNAL_RED_ZONE;
+    if ((word)above < (word)red_zone
+        && (word)(red_zone - above) < 4096)
+      memset(above, 0, (size_t)(red_zone - above));
+
+    /* Components 0 and 1 live in the legacy area: x87, XMM registers.  */
+    if ((xstate_bv & 1) == 0) memset(fp + 32, 0, 128);
+    if ((xstate_bv & 2) == 0) memset(fp + 160, 0, 256);
+    for (i = 2; i < 64; ++i) {
+      unsigned size, offset, ecx, edx;
+
+      if ((xfeatures >> i & 1) == 0 || (xstate_bv >> i & 1) != 0) continue;
+      if (!__get_cpuid_count(0xd, i, &size, &offset, &ecx, &edx)) continue;
+      /* Supervisor components (offset 0) are never in a signal frame.  */
+      if (offset != 0 && offset + size <= xstate_size)
+        memset(fp + offset, 0, size);
+    }
+  }
+#else
+# define GC_clear_signal_frame_slack(context) (void)(context)
+#endif
+
 GC_INLINE void GC_store_stack_ptr(GC_thread me)
 {
   /* There is no data race between the suspend handler (storing         */
@@ -378,6 +448,7 @@
       RESTORE_CANCEL(cancel_state);
       return;
   }
+  GC_clear_signal_frame_slack(context);
   GC_store_stack_ptr(me);
 # ifdef E2K
     GC_ASSERT(NULL == me -> backing_store_end);
